

README file for PALASM 4 version 1.5
----------------------------------------------------------------

Unless otherwise noted the following items apply to both PAL
and MACH parts.

1. Simulation:
    -- Please read section 2.2 in the 1.5 release notes.
    -- Be aware that DATA I/O Unisite only allows 12 input
       changes per vector, even at startup.
    -- If state names are used in the simulation section and
       minimize changes the original polarity, then check clashes
       may occur.
    -- Set and reset are not exchanged for registered pins whose
       polarity is changed by MINIMIZE. Hence, check clashes  in
       simulation may occur when using the "Best For Device",
       "Active-High" or "Active Low"  options in the Logic
       Synthesis submenu.
    -- In-circuit loading of MACH internal registers and observa-
       tion of stored values is not supported at this time.
    -- Simulation for the MACH parts may be inaccurate if there
       are any floating pins in the design file and no PLC file
       has been generated by the FITR prior to invoking the
       simulator.  This is due to the pin could be in any bank
       and so there is no knowing what functional equations
       should be applied to it.
    -- The following parts use the "old simulator":

             16RA8 20RA10 22IP6 23S8 32R16 64R32.

       For such parts, the SIMULATION option under RUN in
       the menu should NOT be selected. If SIMULATION is
       selected, the run will terminate after PARSE. The BOTH
       option under RUN should be selected for these parts.

2. DOS 5.0 & EXTENDED memory PALASM

   To run the extended memory version of PALASM 4 version 1.5
   under DOS 5.0 a memory manager is required:
        - PALASM 4 is not a WINDOWS program ( a separate memory
          manager is required ) and should be run as a
          standalone program.
        - HIMEM.SYS is not sufficient by itself; another memory
          manager is still required. When no other memory manager
          used, BIOS errors are issued by HIMEM's DOS extender
          routines.

   After installation is complete the CONFIG.SYS file should be
   changed to configure the system properly. The following two
   sample configurations provide MS-DOS compatibility when
   used with the Microsoft EMM386  and Quarterdeck Office Systems
   QEMM memory managers.  They have been tested on a system
   configured with most utilities and device drivers in \sys
   directory.

    I. CONFIG.SYS File Using Microsoft EMM386 & HIMEM.SYS:

      DEVICE = C:\SYS\SETVER.EXE
      DEVICE= C:\SYS\HIMEM.SYS
      DOS=high,umb
      device=c:\sys\emm386.exe 2048 m9
      break = on
      buffers = 10
      files =35
      device=c:\sys\smartdrv.sys 2048 1024
      devicehigh=c:\sys\ansi.sys
      shellc:\command.com /e:1512 /p

    II. CONFIG.SYS File Using Quarterdeck QEMM
        ( version 5.13 or higher ) & Microsoft HIMEM.SYS:

      DEVICE = C:\SYS\SETVER.EXE
      DEVICE= C:\QEMM386.SYS RAM MAPS=12 HANDLES=96 NS
      break = on
      buffers = 10
      files =35
      device=c:\loadhi.sys /r:2 c:\sys\smartdrv.sys 2048 1024
      device=c:\loadhi.sys /r:1 c:\sys\ansi.sys
      shellc:\command.com /e:1512 /p

3. KNOWN PROBLEMS
   --- If you have problems with the software, please check Appendix 2
       in the 1.5 release notes.  The problem may already be known and
       a work-around documented.

4. PAIRING PROBLEMS ( MACH PARTS ONLY ):

   --- BPP does not create SET or RESET equations for node
       equations that are input-paired to PIN equations. It is
       mandatory to specify SET/RESET on both of the input-paired
       equations.
   --- A registered/latched pin or node may be output paired by
       BPP with a combinatorial node or pin. This is illegal in
       MACH devices. Such pairings are ignored by the Fitter with
       an appropriate warning. However, simulation timing may be
       affected by the resulting implementation.
   --- BPP may create ( illegal ) duplicate equations when
       either the node or the pin of a PAIR is preplaced and the
       other is floating.

5. MINIMIZE may run out of memory in two ways:
      ---  64K segment problem. In the new MINIMIZE program
           furnished with this release, the 64 segment problem
           does not typically happen unless an equation has many
           product terms ( i.e 25 to 30 ) and each product term
           has only one or two unique signal names.  A sparse
           Karnaugh map results, which probably can't be
           minimized much further anyhow, therefore manually
           "gate split" the offending equation.

      ---  If the extended memory MINIMIZE executable runs out of
           memory, then the user should select the "FAST
           MINIMIZATION" option in the PALASM 4 "LOGIC SYNTHESIS"
           menu. Note, that an "extended" out of memory usually
           happens only when options DT, gate splitting, and
           "Best For Device" ( polarity ) are all set simultaneously.
           Therefore, an alternate strategy to the Fast Minimization
           option is to reduce the number of MINIMIZATION options
           selected. Typically, "Best For Device" is the most
           memory intensive of the options.

       --- When choosing "Best For Device" be aware that if
           Minimize chooses the opposite polarity than the
           original, then when a reset is realized a following
           check on the outputs  will be the opposite value.

6. Parse will give a GP error outside the menu when it detects
   an unusual character (i.e umlauts) rather than give an "un-
   expected character" error, which it does inside the menu.

7. Backannotation ( MACH only ) of truncated names
   ( > 14 characters ) does not work.  Backannotation of
   unused pins and nodes can lead to duplicate pin numbers in
   the design file -- which causes a failure in PARSE.

8. GROUPS:
       - Group names cannot be used within the Simulation section
         to specify values for a collection of signals;
       - GROUP statements in the Declaration section must appear
         after all PIN or NODE statements.

9.  PARSE may run out of memory on rare occasions even in
    extended memory.  Two designs of this type involved very
    complex CASE statements and many STRINGS. The only work-
    around when this occurs in extended memory is to substitute
    the strings manually throughout the design instead of
    allowing the parse program to do it for you.

10. GP errors, ZMALLOC and runtime errors produced by PALASM 4
    programs should be forwarded with your name, phone number
    and .PDS file to AMD Corporate Applications ( 800-222-9323 ).
    Many previously reported errors of this type have been fixed
    in this release.

11. For a 16V8 PAL device, the REG attribute when specified in
    the PIN statement for pin number 16 causes PALXPT to give an
    error ( X3670 ).  Workaround: For pin number 16, remove REG
    from the PIN statement and use := in the equation for the
    pin.

12. If a file is too large to view under the VIEW option of the
    menu, then it may be viewed using the editor by selecting
    the "other file" option under EDITOR and supplying the
    appropriate file name.  Since MACH230 report files are
    larger than for other MACH parts, the "too large to view"
    problem is more likely to occur now than in previous
    releases. Note under the SETUP option the user substitute
    any ascii editor for the PALASM 4 default editor, ED.
    Unfortunately, the DOS 5.0 built-in editor, QEDIT, is not
    compatible with PALASM 4.

13. If you are having diffucties with the extended version running
    on notebooks and laptops (especially TI's), then read the section
    1.3 in the 1.5 release notes.
